-- title:
--   Sec. 4, p. 13 — the BR policy selects $X_{t+1}$ with probability $\bar F(a_{j+1})\mathbb 1(K_t>0)$ given $\mathcal F_t$
-- statement:
--   Under the Budget-Ratio policy, let $t<n$, let $K_t$ be the remaining budget after $t$ decisions, and let $j$ be the index with $T_j\le K_t/(n-t)<T_{j+1}$. Then
--   $$\mathbb P(\sigma^{\mathrm{br}}_{t+1}=1\mid\mathcal F_t)=\bar F(a_{j+1})\,\mathbb 1(K_t>0),$$
--   where $\mathcal F_t$ is generated by $X_1,\dots,X_t$ and $\bar F(a_{j+1})=f_1+\dots+f_j$.
--
--   This is the elementary computation behind the drift of the budget ratio toward a threshold.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Lean index $i$ of `Fin m` is the paper's index $i+1$, so `a 0` is $a_1$, the largest ability. Conditioning on $\mathcal F_t$ is written atom by atom: for every realization $x$, the sum over the value $v$ of the next candidate (Lean coordinate $t$), weighted by $f_v$, of the indicator that BR selects it. $\bar F(a_{j+1})$ is $\sum_{i\le j}f_i$ in Lean indexing.
-- source:
--   Arlotto, Gurvich, Uniformly Bounded Regret in the Multi-Secretary Problem, arXiv:1710.07719v2, Sec. 4, p. 13

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MultiSecretary_BR_Model
import Definitions.Def_MultiSecretary_BR_Policy

namespace MultiSecretary.BR

open Finset

/-- Sec. 4, p. 13: under the BR policy, `P(σ^br_{t+1} = 1 | F_t) = F̄(a_{j+1}) 1(K_t > 0)`, where
`j` is the BR index with `T_j ≤ K_t/(n − t) < T_{j+1}`. Conditioning on `F_t` (the first `t`
abilities, Lean coordinates `0, …, t − 1`) is integrating out the independent coordinate `t`
against `f`; `F̄(a_{j+1}) = f_1 + ⋯ + f_j` is `∑_{i ≤ j} f i` in Lean indexing. -/
theorem br_selection_probability {m : ℕ} (I : Instance m) (n k : ℕ) (x : Fin n → Fin m)
    (t : Fin n) :
    ∑ v : Fin m, I.f v * (if I.brPolicy n k (Function.update x t v) t = true then 1 else 0) =
      if 0 < I.budget n k x t.val then
        ∑ i ∈ univ.filter (fun i => i ≤ I.brIndex (I.ratio n k x t.val)), I.f i
      else 0 := by sorry

end MultiSecretary.BR
